// Global-area declaration syntax the parser cannot accept.
//
// Parse-stage only. Split from core-parse-fns because the global area recovers
// by skipping to the next statement, and a malformed variable declaration
// leaves enough behind to be misread as a second bad global statement.

// Only declarations belong in the global area
3 + 4                  //~ ErrorBadGloStmt:1 "Invalid global area statement"

// A declaration needs a name. What is left of the line is then neither a
// finished statement nor a declaration.
mut = 5                //~ ErrorNoIdent:5 "Expected variable name for declaration"
                       //~^ ErrorNoSemi:5 follow-on
                       //~^^ ErrorBadGloStmt:5 follow-on

// A declaration names one storage location that only it owns, so the aliasing
// distinctions the reference permissions draw say nothing about it. What is
// left is whether the value may change, which 'mut' and 'imm' say and the other
// four do not. Same rule on a field: struct-parse holds it there. 'ro' used to
// be accepted here and nowhere else, as the spelling of a constant, which
// 'const' already is.
ro readonly = 1        //~ ErrorInvType:1 "must be 'mut' or 'imm'"
uni sole = 2           //~ ErrorInvType:1 "must be 'mut' or 'imm'"

// A const declaration must supply its value. The diagnostic lands on the token
// that should have been '=', which is the start of the next declaration, so
// this may not be the file's last declaration.
const MISSING
mut afterConst = 1     //~ ErrorNoInit:1 "Must specify const value"
